{"title": "CSCI-632: Mobile Robot Programming Foundations", "learningObjectives": [
    "Define the core software architecture components of a mobile robot system, including sensing, state estimation, planning, control, and actuation.",
    "Explain how ROS 2 message contracts, coordinate transforms, and timestamped observations enable reliable robot software communication.",
    "Apply reactive motion control and path planning concepts to simulate and evaluate robot navigation in dynamic environments."
  ], "beats": [
    {
      "title": "Robot Software Architecture Overview",
      "claim": "Robot software separates sensing, state estimation, planning, control, and actuation while making message contracts and timing assumptions explicit.",
      "example": "A navigation stack uses a perception node to publish camera data, a localization node to estimate pose, a planner to generate waypoints, a controller to compute velocities, and an actuator driver to send commands to wheels.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a layered block diagram showing five stacked modules (sensing, state estimation, planning, control, actuation) with bidirectional arrows indicating data flow and explicit timing labels on each interface.",
      "sourceIds": ["ros-2-concepts"]
    },
    {
      "title": "ROS 2 Node Communication Model",
      "claim": "ROS 2 organizes a robot application as nodes that communicate through typed interfaces, where topics support publish-subscribe streams, services support request-response, and actsa",
      "example": "A localization node subscribes to a topic publishing IMU data, while a planning service handles a request-response call to compute a path between two waypoints.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a network diagram with labeled nodes connected by color-coded lines representing topics (dashed), services (solid), and actions (dotted), with message type tags on each line.",
      "sourceIds": ["ros-2-concepts"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Parameter Configuration and Behavior",
      "claim": "Parameters configure node behavior, allowing runtime adjustment of thresholds, gains, and operational limits without recompiling code.",
      "example": "A controller node uses a parameter named 'max_speed' to cap wheel velocity, which can be updated via a ROS 2 parameter server during simulation.",
      "visualDirection": "Render a configuration panel with key-value pairs for robot parameters, highlighting how changing a single value propagates to the corresponding node's behavior settings.",
      "sourceIds": ["ros-2-concepts"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Coordinate Transforms and Reference Frames",
      "claim": "Coordinate transforms describe relationships among reference frames, enabling accurate mapping of sensor data to the robot's global coordinate system.",
      "example": "A camera's local image coordinates are transformed into the robot's base link frame using a rotation matrix and translation vector to align with the map.",
      "visualDirection": "Illustrate two overlapping coordinate grids (local and global) with labeled axes, showing a transformation arrow and matrix notation connecting them.",
      "sourceIds": ["ros-2-concepts"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sensor Timestamping and Frame Interpretation",
      "claim": "Sensors produce observations that must be timestamped and interpreted in the correct frame to maintain synchronization across the system.",
      "example": "LiDAR scans are tagged with a precise timestamp and attached to the robot's odometry frame, ensuring they align temporally with camera and IMU data.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a timeline with synchronized sensor events, each marked with a timestamp and frame label, connected to a central synchronization node.",
      "sourceIds": ["ros-2-concepts"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Reactive Motion Control Principles",
      "claim": "Reactive motion control adjusts robot behavior in real time based on immediate environmental feedback, enabling safe navigation in dynamic settings.",
      "example": "A velocity controller reduces forward speed when a proximity sensor detects an obstacle within two meters, preventing collisions without full replanning.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a side-view simulation of a robot approaching a wall, with a reactive control loop highlighted, displaying input sensor data, computed velocity, and output wheel commands.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-632"]
    },
    {

      "title": "Map Building and Localization",
      "claim": "Map building and localization enable robots to construct environmental models and determine their position within those models using sensor data.",
      "example": "A SLAM algorithm fuses wheel odometry and camera images to update a grid map, then uses feature matching to locate the robot's current pose.",
      "visualDirection": "Render a top-down map grid with a moving robot icon, showing how sensor observations incrementally fill in map cells and update the robot's position marker.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-632"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Path Planning in Dynamic Environments",
      "claim": "Path planning algorithms compute sequences of actions that guide robots from a start to a goal while avoiding obstacles and adapting to changes.",
      "example": "A reactive planner recalculates a route when a moving pedestrian enters the robot's path, inserting a detour that maintains safety margins and task completion time.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a navigation map with a planned path, a dynamic obstacle, and a revised path, using color coding to distinguish static obstacles, dynamic hazards, and planned trajectories.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-632"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Simulation and Physical Deployment",
      "claim": "Students implement various algorithms in simulation as well as on a real robot, allowing iterative testing before physical deployment.",
      "example": "A navigation algorithm is first validated in a physics engine simulation, then deployed on a wheeled robot to verify real-world performance and adjust parameters accordingly.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a split-screen comparison: left side shows a simulation environment with debug overlays, right side shows a physical robot with sensor data feeds and control interfaces.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-632"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist",
      "claim": "Effective robot software development requires systematic evaluation of latency, dropped messages, sensor noise, actuator limits, safety boundaries, and simulation-to-physical gaps.",
      "example": "A developer checks that message latency stays below 50 milliseconds, verifies sensor noise does not exceed calibration thresholds, and confirms safety zones are enforced before field testing.",
      "visualDirection": "Present a checklist graphic with five categories (latency, message integrity, sensor noise, actuator limits, safety boundaries), each with a pass/fail indicator and a brief validation method.",
      "sourceIds": ["ros-2-concepts"]
    }
  ]}
